<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ferdinand Christian Baur</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1792">1792</a>–<a href="/years/1860">1860</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ferdinand Christian Baur was a German Protestant theologian, professor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tubingen">Tübingen</a> from 1826 and founder of the Tübingen school of biblical criticism. Applying Hegelian ideas of historical development, he interpreted early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> as a conflict between Jewish and Gentile parties and studied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> writings as products of their time. His approach shaped modern historical theology.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ferdinand-christian-baur.jpg" alt="Ferdinand Christian Baur" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: After Christoph Friedrich Doerr · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Christoph_F_D%C3%B6rr_-_Dr_FC_von_Baur_-_Stahlstich_1830er.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ferdinand Christian Baur was born on 21 June 1792 at Schmiden, near Cannstatt. After the theological seminary of Blaubeuren he entered the Tübinger Stift in 1809, where he studied under Ernst Bengel while the philosophers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-gottlieb-fichte">Fichte</a> and Schelling made a deep impression on him. In 1817 he returned to Blaubeuren as a professor, a turning point that began the investigations on which his reputation rests. His first important work, Symbolik und Mythologie oder die Naturreligion des Altertums, appeared in 1824 and 1825 and showed the influence of Schelling and Friedrich Schleiermacher. In 1826 he was called to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tubingen">Tübingen</a> as professor of theology.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>At Tübingen Baur wrote on the Manichaean religious system (1831), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollonius-of-tyana">Apollonius of Tyana</a> (1832), Christian Gnosis (1835) and the Christian element in Platonism (1837). He exchanged Schleiermacher for Hegel and adopted Hegel's philosophy of history, saying that without philosophy history was for him dead and dumb. His essay of 1831 on the Christ party at Corinth argued that Paul was opposed there by a Jewish Christian party.</p><p>In 1835 he argued that the Pastoral Epistles were written in the middle of the second century against Gnostics, and in his Paulus of 1845 he held that only Galatians, 1 and 2 Corinthians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> were genuinely Pauline, and that the Paul of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-the-apostles">Acts of the Apostles</a> differs from the Paul of the letters.</p><p>His underlying thesis was that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> grew out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> and had to overcome Jewish limits before becoming universal. The antagonism between the Jewish apostles Peter, James and John and Paul, the apostle to the Gentiles, lasted, he held, until the middle of the second century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> arose from their reconciliation.</p><p>In 1847 he applied this to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a>, ranking Matthew closest to an original Gospel, then Luke and Mark, and treating John as idealistic and not historical. He also wrote histories of dogma, among them a three-volume history of dogma, Die christliche Lehre von der Dreieinigkeit und Menschwerdung Gottes (1841 to 1843), and a defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a> against Johann Möhler in 1834.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Baur died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tubingen">Tübingen</a> aged 68. His Kirchengeschichte appeared in five volumes between 1853 and 1863, partly by himself and partly by his son Ferdinand and his son-in-law Eduard Zeller from his notes and lectures. Otto Pfleiderer called it a classic and the first thorough attempt to explain the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> on strictly historical lines.</p><p>His school was at its height in the 1840s, with Adolf Hilgenfeld and Albert Schwegler among its members. It then lost ground, as English scholars showed that the late dates it gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> documents were impossible, and it has been generally abandoned since Adolf von Harnack proposed very early dates for the synoptic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> and Acts around 1910.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ferdinand Christian Baur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The University of Tübingen and its Protestant seminary trained clergy, officials and scholars, and the theologian <b>Ferdinand Christian Baur</b> and his pupil David Friedrich Strauss, whose Life of Jesus of 1835 caused a sensation, made the Tübingen school central to modern biblical criticism.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wurttemberg">Kingdom of Württemberg · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Fellbach, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tubingen">Tübingen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.81,9.28&amp;year=1836" aria-label="Fellbach on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4765 459 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ferdinand Christian Baur"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5215 729h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5215 729h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.81,9.28&amp;year=1836">What was here in 1836? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
